WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
You might have heard people say that AI gets things wrong, and then

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.080
it makes mistakes.

3
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.120
But why is that?

4
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.920
By the end of this video, you'll have an idea why, and how to guide it correctly.

5
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.980
To do so, we'll start by talking about a light switch.

6
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.500
It's a pretty simple and familiar thing.

7
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.840
You flick it on, and the light comes on.

8
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.540
That is how old-fashioned computer code works.

9
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.560
The same action gives the same result, with no surprises.

10
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.680
But AI is different.

11
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.240
It's much more like rolling a dice.

12
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.620
It's not magic, it's not human, and it's not thinking.

13
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.080
It is a machine.

14
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.260
It's working out probabilities using some clever maths, and then

15
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.220
picking one of the most likely answers.

16
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.040
Let's imagine a dice, and that we want to roll a six.

17
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.000
With a normal dice, we roll and we hope.

18
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.340
Most of the time we don't get a six, but now and then we do.

19
00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:01.540
Real AI does far better than that, but it can still be wrong in a similar way.

20
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.460
And this is the root of how AI gets things wrong.

21
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.380
Not because it's broken, but because the dice landed on the wrong side.

22
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.300
Now imagine a shop that sells specialist dice.

23
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.100
Dice with a six on every side.

24
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.000
Roll one of these, and it can only land on a six.

25
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.400
That is what you're doing when you're trying to tell an AI clearly what you want.

26
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.680
Every clear, relevant thing you say paints another side with a six.

27
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.875
Now, our real AI dice doesn't have six sides, it has a hundred

28
00:01:33.875 --> 00:01:35.000
thousand sides or more.

29
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.540
For every word that it writes, or even part of a word that it

30
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.020
writes, it rolls again.

31
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.320
You'll never manage to paint every side with your figurative sixes,

32
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.220
but the more you paint, the better your odds are of rolling a six.

33
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.180
But there's a catch.

34
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.060
Imagine you grew up in a remote desert island.

35
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.620
Your whole world is palm trees, sunshine and fish.

36
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.860
You do have one book though, and it's all about polar bears.

37
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.500
One day someone comes along and asks you about the North Pole.

38
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.620
Well, you certainly know a few things, but you have no real idea what it's like.

39
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.620
All you can picture is a cold desert island with large, white,

40
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.220
extremely hungry, fluffy mammals.

41
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.320
AI has the same problem.

42
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.760
If it hasn't been told something, it fills the gap with whatever seems likely.

43
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.440
And it won't say "I don't know".

44
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.500
Instead, it will guess as if it is the right answer, because it

45
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.480
doesn't know enough to know it's wrong.

46
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.700
Picture two inspectors walking round the same building, using the

47
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.240
same checklist and the same AI system.

48
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.900
One gets a report that's spot on, first time.

49
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.940
The other spends half an hour putting it right.

50
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.500
The difference isn't the AI.

51
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.220
It's how they talk.

52
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.620
One says "the door's closer is broken".

53
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.040
Which door?

54
00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.160
The AI has to guess.

55
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.800
The other says "kitchen, second fire door, third floor, the door's

56
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.520
closer is broken".

57
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.980
There's nothing to guess.

58
00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:06.580
It's clear.

59
00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.800
There are three habits that make Quickler work better.

60
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.440
These are essentially the same as painting sixes on our dice.

61
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.840
The first two are for when we set things up.

62
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.160
First, make sure your questions are precise and distinct.

63
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.820
If two questions ask nearly the same thing, even a person would

64
00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.960
struggle to know which box an answer belongs in.

65
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.640
An AI struggles here as well.

66
00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:37.680
Secondly, we can use section headings as signposts, like Fire doors,

67
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.500
Lighting and Kitchen.

68
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:44.220
They help the AI, just as it would help a person, to work out where something goes.

69
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.300
Third is for when you're on site, and it's the main one.

70
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.120
As you go round, talk about what you're seeing, and be specific.

71
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.260
Don't just send photos or information on their own.

72
00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.880
Something like "the fire extinguisher by the main stairs is in date,

73
00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.220
and the pin and seal are in good order" is a great help.

74
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.220
If you send a photo, there's no knowing what it means, even for a

75
00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.060
human, unless there is text that can be read in the photo.

76
00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.880
In our own data, about a third of photos arrive with no words at all.

77
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.600
Think of a new apprentice on their first day, and you tell them "go

78
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.980
and sort that out", and neither of you really knows what will happen

79
00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:28.880
next, because no one is entirely sure what that is.

80
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.640
But if we tell the apprentice "replace the cracked tile by door

81
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.140
three", you'll get a replaced cracked tile by door three.

82
00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.260
AI is the same.

83
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.740
Say where you are first, and say what you are seeing.

84
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.940
"Room sixteen, the windows have damaged seals."

85
00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.680
If we were to walk in somewhere and just say "damaged seals",

86
00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.700
there's no context; there's no knowing if that is a damaged window

87
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.460
seal, a door seal or a sea mammal.

88
00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.260
This is because AI isn't replacing your expertise.

89
00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.340
That expertise is in your head, and the only way to get it out of

90
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.140
your head is for you to speak properly.

91
00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.620
What all of this allows is for you to walk around and do your

92
00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.700
inspection naturally.

93
00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.740
Most inspection apps are like a lift, where you go from floor to

94
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.560
floor in a set order.

95
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.440
Question one, question two, question three.

96
00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.620
In order, one at a time.

97
00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.140
But that's not natural.

98
00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:28.340
In reality, you walk into a classroom and see the floor, the walls

99
00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.530
and the call points all at once.

100
00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.440
Quickler lets you work that way.

101
00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.060
It works a little like a mail room.

102
00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:39.620
It tips out the whole sack, in other words all of your messages,

103
00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:44.480
reads every address, and puts every letter in the right pigeonhole.

104
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.600
With the extra benefit that if you said "no defects" in the vans,

105
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.200
and ten minutes later spotted a broken wing mirror, you can just say

106
00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.460
so, and the latest answer wins.

107
00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:59.360
Of course, there are some things that must never be left to AI, and

108
00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.020
Quickler doesn't leave everything to AI.

109
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.640
Many things are covered by our figurative light switches, and they

110
00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.100
work the same way every time.

111
00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.540
So next time you're on site, say what you see, be specific, and talk

112
00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:13.860
about your photos.

113
00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.840
Be the inspector who is precise in their language.

114
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:21.100
And in that way, you are painting your dice with sixes, and you're

115
00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.160
going to roll a six.
